its age and  it killed about 38 people on a kind of  ordinary work day at the corner of wall  and broad streets in New York we've  largely forgotten it but at the time it  was this huge deal and this huge  investigation in the very early years of  the FBI and so Hoover is not director at  that point but he's a very young man  kind of working behind the scenes  beginning to think about the issue on  the one hand of terrorism but more  importantly at that moment of political  radicalism and particularly left-wing  radicalism in the United States and so I  encountered him in doing the work on  that project really as a very young man  and what was striking to me was that  there he is he's in his mid 20s at that  point and he has not yet become the  figure that we are all going to get to  know but he's already thinking through  so many of these issues that he's going  to become identified with later on  namely in that case the kind of war  against communism which so much shapes  American politics 90th century how does  your book differ from the many other  books out there about Hoover right well  there are a couple of things that I  think make this book different so Hoover  is someone who's been written about  obviously a great deal i think he is  still a household name for the most part  although when i talk with my students  they know they've heard of him they're  not always a hundred percent sure which  Hoover he is right vacuumer since right  is he recent meeting a little bit are we  still enough enough of a name so anyway  he's been written about obviously for a  long time by lots of people but a lot of  the tradition of writing about Hoover  has been very much a tradition of expose  so most of the big biographies of Hoover  have been books that are seeking to kind  of expose his secrets tell us things  that people didn't know at the time that  he was actually in political power so  that's part of what my book is as well  but it's also trying to  we step back from that a little bit and  assess Hoover not only as someone who's  keeping secrets but someone who plays  this very big role in American politics  and to kind of take him from from the  fringes and this idea of him as a rogue  villain and really put him back at the  center of our political story so I'd say  that's that's piece one not trying to  rewrite that picture of him a little bit  the second thing that the book is doing  is trying to put him in conversation  with really interesting work that's  being done on the kind of history and  evolution of American conservatism in  the 20th century and a lot of that work  tends to kind of look at a resurgence of  grassroots conservatism that begins to  happen in the 60s and 70s and then  ultimately comes of age with Ronald  Reagan and the Reagan presidency in the  80s and this is an effort to recast that  a little bit and to think about someone  like Hoover as an ideological  conservative but an ideological  conservative who holds a whole lot of  state and government power through the  period that we usually think about as  the great age of American liberalism so  again it's sort of recasting him in a  different relationship with American  politics than I think that we've seen  before okay and in doing your research  and I would imagine part of the one of  the big issues is there so much written  about him but you were able to uncover  some things that no other historian has  seen before and also you gain to access  to some files that had never or  collections private collections that had  never been opened before how did you how  did that come to pass and what were some  of your findings I just talked about  Europe you know how you did the research  in general well I'd say the blessing and  the curse of doing research on an  institution like the FBI is that there's  always more to find so that makes it  terrific because there's always material  out there that people haven't really  looked at and haven't scrutinized in a  serious way well in my case is that  though how is that that no one has come  to seen it of  are the files just being made open to  the public is that is that the situation  or there's just so much that it's  impossible for one person to go through  it all right well it's kind of a  combination so this the material that's  at the FBI is constantly being processed  and sent to the National Archives but  we're talking about millions millions of  pages of material something that no  single person could possibly go through  so to some degree you can go to the  National Archives and begin to look  through this material that's been  recently released a lot of the FBI's  material and even pretty far back  historical material even back as early  as the teens of the 20s some of that is  still at the FBI and almost everything  from the 50s 60s and 70s is still at the  FBI and can be acquired through the  Freedom of Information Act and through  filing Freedom of Information Act  requests so what this means is that you  know in my case I happen to be  particularly interested in the FBI's  relationship with conservative  organizations and so I began to file  Freedom of Information Act requests on a  whole variety of conservative  organizations that you know for the most  part people just haven't filed for your  requests about before and so therefore  the material hasn't come to light so  doing research there is a little bit  different because you can't just go and  sit down and have all of it there you  have to actually be rather proactive  about finding it the other question of  course is a redaction which is to say  crossing out the names of people and  also declassification which happens over  time so by the very nature of the  passage of time under our current laws  it means that there's always new  material coming up new material being  released I think the last and really  interesting thing that's happening is  that the generation of people who knew  Hoover in the 60s and 70s or who were  officials with him etc many of those  people have become quite elderly or have  died over the last 40 years and so their  private collections have also begun to  come to light and have been gathered  together in a serious way for instance L  Patrick gray who was Hoover successor at  the FBI very briefly  sort of between the time that Hoover  died in 1972 and the time that the kind  of Watergate scandal really began to  emerge in 73 and 74 he's there in this  very critical moment and his son has  actually gathered together a lot of his  papers and pulled together a pretty  serious collection that hasn't been very  widely used so what if some of the  things that you were able to find that  you know no one has seen before for  instance or anything in particular that  was surprising that you came across  right well in terms of some of the  documents about you know Hoover's  relationship with with important  conservative figures the conservative  movement I think you can see a real set  of kind of serious ideological back and  forth that's going on there I think we  tend to have an image of Hoover as being  kind of a rogue figure who's getting  what he wants by blackmailing people  writing I mean certainly this is to some  degree at least in part of what he did  but one of the things that I think  that's interesting at looking at these  these documents that haven't been  studied a lot is to see what a careful  architect he was about building a pretty  widespread political grassroots base for  the FBI through these organizations so  everything from women's clubs to the  American Legion to more kind of overt  overtly conservative grassroots  organizations you can actually see his  skills I think as a politician in that  sense in ways that aren't as often  recognized and then there are all sorts  of you know just sort of interesting  little anecdotes that come up in the  papers as well ways that he deals with  the press I mean particularly Hoover as  a young 