er  scare right which sort of parallels the  Red Scare particularly in the 40s and  50s in which homosexuality is understood  to be a national security threat and so  it's a very perplexing situation here is  a man that many people understand to at  least be suggestively homosexual right  who is militantly also cracking down on  homosexuals and so I think we have to  understand this both as as a kind of  defense of his own position and I think  more interestingly  see the ways in which he crafts the FBI  as a kind of defense against these  accusations in part right a very very  masculine kind of male centered  institution right and and with a sort of  very interesting and distinct public  image this image of the g-man that  emerges in this moment I think one of  the things that's going to be really fun  to play with in the in the final version  of the book are these these  representations of a certain kind of  masculinity how people understood that  then and how we want to understand it  now how do you think that Hoover in that  span of 50 years shaped the American  political culture well I think Hoover  did a couple of things so one thing is  that he though we tend to as I said  again think of him as this kind of rogue  figure you know in many ways he is  adopting what are the essential kind of  liberal techniques of State Building  government efficiency kind of insulating  administrative managers and bureaucrats  from the evil influences of politics all  of these things which often progressives  embraced which certainly the New Deal  embraced in many ways the strengthening  of the federal government all of these  things that we tend to think of as being  somehow inherently liberal or  progressive or often framed in that way  and the way we talk about American  politics he sort of takes these tools of  State Building and uses them to build a  very different part of of the American  state and of the American government so  I think he's very interesting as a kind  of bureaucratic architect and as it as a  kind of case study in that in terms of  his political influence I do think that  he was one of the most important people  certainly within the government itself  in sort of containing and limiting the  left in the 20th century and I think in  some ways this is one of the biggest  messages of the book is as I said I talk  about him as  a conservative who held a whole lot of  government power in an age when we tend  to think of as the age of liberalism  right this kind of mid-century moment  and one of the things that he spent a  lot of his life doing is watching and  policing kind of radical movements on  the left and I think in the end kind of  containing and shaping American culture  both through kind of policing left and  through very overtly promoting a  conservative anti-communist message that  had great influence and resonance in the  United States okay and your book will be  out in uh 2014-15 so I got a few more  years of working well we will certainly  watch for that because I think it will  be a very interesting read thank you so  much for being here today with us and  sharing some of your work great thanks  Marilyn for more information about  professor gauge and her research please  visit our website at yale edu slash  mcmillan report be sure to join us again  for another episode of the macmillan  report made possible through funding  from the Whitney and Betty Macmillan  Center for International and area  studies at Yale  you

